.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS decode
.EX
void decode(u8[] text, reflect.Value v, i64 flags = 0);
T decode<T>(u8[] text, i64 flags = 0);
.EE
.PP
Fill v (reflect.of(x)) from the JSON in text. Strings, slices and the structs pointers point to are allocated: json.free(v) frees them. Throws ERR_DECODE when text isn't JSON or doesn't fit v's type, with where: "1:34: lines[1].price: expected a number, got a string". The text is read front to back by a json.Reader, no tree is built, and a @json struct (at any depth) is read by its generated fromJson.
.PP
The JSON in text as a T: \fBUser u = json.decode<User>(text);\fR, freed with \fBjson.free(reflect.of(u))\fR\&. Fields missing from the text are zero. Throws ERR_DECODE as decode does; what was filled in by then is freed first, so there is nothing to clean up after a throw.
.SS free
.EX
void free(reflect.Value v);
.EE
.PP
Free what decode allocated in v: its Strings, the items of its slices and vec.Vecs, its u8 * strings and what its other pointers point to, through nested structs and arrays; they are zeroed. Only for values decode filled (or that own those the same way). Private fields are left alone.
