.SS select
.EX
Value *select(Value *j, u8 *fmt, ...args);
.EE
.PP
Allows selections like:
.PP
Json \fIJ = json.parse(text); Json *width = json.select(J,".foo.bar[\fR].baz",4);      // [\fI] takes an i64 Json *height = json.select(J,".tv.type[4].\fR","name");  // .* takes a u8 * Json \fIprice = json.select(J,".clothes.shirt[4].price_\fR","eur");
.PP
You can also include a :<type> specifier, typically at the end, to verify the type of the final JSON object selected. If the type doesn't match, the function will return null. For instance, the specifier .foo.bar:s will return null unless the root object has a 'foo' field, which is an object with a 'bar' field that contains a string. Here is a comprehensive list of selectors:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
   ".field" selects the "field" of the current object (the first one if
            the key repeats).
   "[1234]" selects the specified index of the current array.
   ":<type>" checks if the currently selected type is of the specified type,
             where the type can be a single letter representing:
     "s" \-> string
     "f" \-> float
     "i" \-> int
     "a" \-> array
     "o" \-> object
     "b" \-> boolean
     "!" \-> null
.EE
.RE
.SS readerNew
.EX
Reader readerNew(u8[] text, i64 flags = 0);
.EE
.PP
A Reader at the start of text. With json.STRICT in flags, generated fromJson methods throw on a key with no field and on a missing key.
.SS writerNew
.EX
Writer writerNew(i64 indent = 0);
.EE
.PP
An empty Writer. With indent > 0 every member and item goes on its own line, indented that many spaces per level, and keys are followed by ": "; with 0 (the default) the text is compact: {"a":1,"b":[2,3]}
